# Boy Scout rule

Two duties on every create/edit batch:

1. **You (parent agent)** — leave touched files better (local cleanup).
2. **Sub-agent (required)** — at the **end** of the modification batch, score those files and write `Quality: N/10` headers.

Do **not** self-assign final Quality scores in the parent turn. The scorer sub-agent owns the numbers.

---

## 1. Parent: Boy Scout cleanup (during the edit)

For each file you create or change, do **one** nearby improvement in the same edit, for example:

- Remove dead code / obsolete comments
- Fix a misleading name next to your change
- Refresh Intent / Architecture if the role shifted
- Dedupe a helper you just touched
- Drop an unused import

**Limits:** only files required for the task; no repo-wide cleanups; no formatting-only drive-bys; no deleting tests to “simplify.”

---

## 2. Sub-agent: Quality scoring (end of every modif)

After your code/docs edits for the user request are done (and tests you owed are run), **always** launch a **Task** sub-agent before your final user reply.

### How to launch

Use the `Task` tool:

- `subagent_type`: `generalPurpose` (or `explore` if read-only scoring + header writes are enough and the model can edit)
- `model`: `fast` when available (scoring is narrow)
- `description`: `Score MyChat file quality`
- Prefer **one** sub-agent per turn covering **all** files created/modified in this batch (not one agent per file)

### Sub-agent prompt (use this shape)

```text
You are the MyChat Boy Scout Quality scorer. Independent of the parent’s optimism.

Repo: /Users/nicolasmarchand/Documents/Dev/Projects/MyChat

Files to score (created or modified this turn):
- <absolute or repo-relative path>
- …

For EACH file:
1. Read the full file (or enough to judge structure).
2. Read any existing `Quality: N/10 — …` line.
3. Assign an honest score with the scale below.
4. Upsert the Quality line in the correct header place (do not invent Intent/Architecture if missing—only add Quality; if Intent/Architecture exist, keep them and put Quality after).
5. If a prior score exists, you may raise it only when the file clearly improved; never inflate. You may lower it if the edit made things worse.
6. Note must be short and concrete (what blocks a higher score, or why it’s strong).

Header placement:
- JS/CSS/Python: top module comment → `* Quality: N/10 — note`
- HTML: `<!-- Quality: N/10 — note -->` near top
- Cursor `.mdc`: HTML comment right after YAML frontmatter
- Markdown README: HTML comment at top

Scale:
1–3 hard to follow / mixed concerns / dangerous
4–5 works but messy or oversized
6–7 solid; a few concrete smells
8–9 clean and easy to change; minor nits
10 reserved — essentially nothing useful left in-file

Return to parent: a bullet list `path → N/10 — note` only. No refactors beyond the Quality line (and fixing a malformed existing Quality line).
```

### After the sub-agent returns

- Briefly trust its scores; do not rewrite them upward in the parent.
- If the sub-agent failed to launch, say so in one line and apply best-effort scores yourself as fallback (exception only).

---

## ❌ Avoid

- Parent inventing final `Quality` scores “to save a turn”
- Scoring files you did not touch
- Sub-agent doing feature work or broad refactors
- Raising scores without evidence in the file contents
